// The services PoolWatch knows and offers to turn off. "lose", "risk" and "note" are PoolWatch's own words.
//
// Each Windows row says where the advice to turn it off comes from ("source"):
//   wdot  Microsoft's Windows Desktop Optimization Tool,
//         https://github.com/The-Virtual-Desktop-Team/Windows-Desktop-Optimization-Tool, the file
//         Configurations/Templates/Services.json (Configurations/2009 is the same) at commit ff2574c (2026-05-20).
//         MIT licence, plus the sample-code terms in the tool's EULA.txt, provided "as is". The tool is written for virtual
//         desktops. Its file lists the services it can turn off; each ships set to "Skip", and the tool turns off those its
//         user sets to "Apply".
//   cis   CIS Microsoft Windows 11 Enterprise Benchmark v5.1.0, section 5 (System Services), a security checklist for
//         company PCs. Cited by item number and service only, never its text (the benchmark is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0).
//   both  both of them; none  neither (a row kept from before the sources were added).
// Neither source says a service is safe to turn off on a person's own PC. Check every row again for each release.
// Both sources set what they turn off to Disabled, and so does a row with a source, unless its note says why not.
// A row is never added without a source; the vendor rows have none and are for the AI-agent route.
